window.COACH = {"page": "model", "title": "Math Model", "prizeName": "Best Model", "summary": {"verdict": "Against the six Best Model winners in the casebook, this page is strongest exactly where it counts: it names the no-weather county trend as the no-skill line any weather model must beat, runs a prospective blind test frozen on 26 September, labels every input in the dose chain Measured, Assumed or Arithmetic, and publishes a standard-curve error that cuts its own activity numbers by 46%. Its problem is that it disagrees with itself in public. One dose appears as both 4,008 and 4,082 L/ha, the abstract sheet calls the index Validated where the Block 1 conclusion refuses that word, the stomatal densities in Figures 25 and 26 contradict the sentence that cites them, and the payback table does not scale with its own efficacy column. Clear those and the modelling will speak for itself.", "strengths": ["Block 1 is scored on data it never saw, against a named no-skill baseline, with both errors printed side by side and the blind test refused as too early to call.", "The dose table marks every input Measured, Assumed or Arithmetic, including \"Plate area | 100 cm2 | Reconstructed, never written down\". No winner labels its own measurements against its borrowings this clearly in one view.", "The page publishes a calibration error that reduces every derived activity by a factor of 1.460, plus an errata section, and reports its titre against a derived limit of detection and limit of quantitation.", "Three section conclusions state what the block establishes and then the claim it does not support, in the same paragraph. The Block 1 and Block 2 conclusions are the clearest writing on the page.", "The salinity against waterlogging mismatch is published as the largest open problem in the model, with the cost of fixing it quoted at about 15 applications over a 30-day window."], "priorities": ["Settle 4,082 against 4,008 L/ha. Keep 4,082, the unrounded 2.444 canopy chain, then re-export Figures 20, 21 and 22, the land-size table and the four-basis table, change the abstract sheet, and delete the sentence that explains the gap.", "Repair the three sentences that contradict their own figures and tables: the stomatal densities under \"Enzyme entering the leaf\", the absorbance rank in \"Signal is not a biomass artefact\", and \"a known area\" against the plate area the table calls Assumed.", "Make the abstract sheet agree with the conclusions. Validated, Upper bound and \"each tested against something outside itself\" are all withdrawn later on the page, and the sheet is what a judge reads first.", "Repair the payback arithmetic: NT$4,429 against the table's 4,635, the ordinary-paddy rows that do not scale with their own efficacy column, and \"an efficacy of 12.8\". This block is the page's headline result and all three are checkable in under a minute.", "Give the page one top-level Limitations section and one errata section, and give 32%, 0.28 and the 29 min doubling time a source on the page."], "ballot": [{"question": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?", "status": "strong", "where": "Block 1 Results 1 to 3, and Block 3 Result 3", "note": "A 32,767-model out-of-sample search, a stage-weighted yield model with a sign constraint, an inverted Hill transfer function and an economic block no Best Model winner attempts."}, {"question": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?", "status": "strong", "where": "Block 3 Result 3 and the page Conclusion", "note": "The 4.5 day lead time against a 3-day forecast changed how the reactor would be run, from chasing weather to brewing stock, and the payback split sent the project toward salt-affected coastal land."}, {"question": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?", "status": "strong", "where": "Block 1 Result 3, Block 2 Result 3, Block 3 Result 2", "note": "Twenty-five years of county harvests, a frozen 2026 forecast, our own DNPH assay and our own Arabidopsis dose. The weakness is weight, not absence: two of the three blocks rest on one well and one plate, and the page says so."}, {"question": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?", "status": "partial", "where": "References", "note": "The errata, the provenance labels and the no-skill baseline are exemplary habits, but there is no repository, no fitting code, no release of the 32,767-model scoring run and no software versions, so nobody can rerun any of it."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["One chain: weather goes in, litres of protectant per hectare come out", "Block 1 · Stress index", "Block 2 · Optogenetic kinetics", "Block 3 · Dose and payback", "Conclusion", "References"], "winners": ["A 100 to 400 word lead: the problem, the cost of solving it at the bench, the model named, and what it takes in and gives out", "A scope or assumptions declaration near the top", "Methods before results, with tool choices justified and not just listed", "Results in blocks, each block ending in a stated consequence", "A comparison against data the model did not contain", "Limitations, next steps, references"], "gap": "We hit five of the six beats and we order by the journey of the thing being modelled, which the casebook calls the most readable of the six orderings. Two beats are missing. There is no scope declaration near the top: the chain cards say what each block takes and gives but never what it assumes. And there is no limitations section of its own, because ours is a fold inside Block 3 that covers Block 3 only."}, "extras": ["The first-crop index table repeats itself: rows 0.20 and 0.25 both read \"−1.8% | −129 | −3,311\", and the \"Chance of losing > 5%\" column runs 2, 12, 25, 35, 25, 32, 45, which falls between 0.20 and 0.25. Recompute the two rows, or say in the footnote why risk drops as the index rises.", "Two green wavelengths. Block 2 opens with \"Green light at 535 nm activates the sensor kinase CcaS\" and the lux conversion uses \"photon energy at 526 nm\", which Figure 15 repeats. Say which is the CcaS peak and which is the lamp, in both places.", "Assumption 4 reads \"should be modeled separately\". Everywhere else the page writes modelled and modelling. Change it to \"modelled\".", "The lead writes \"vapor pressure deficit\" and the Figure 1 artwork writes \"vapour pressure deficit\". Pick one spelling for the page.", "The Figure 1 title reads \"Targeted Foliar AccD Delivery\" while the text uses ACCD and the gene is acdS. Use ACCD in the artwork, or define the three forms once.", "\"7.2514 mU mL−1\" in the dose table and the References gives four decimal places to a single well below the limit of quantitation. Quote it as 7.25 everywhere, or attach a spread.", "Three assumption schemes on one page: 1 to 5 in Block 1, B1 to B3 in Block 2, and an unnumbered prose fold in Block 3. Number them the same way and give Block 3's fold its own numbered list.", "Assumption 5's reason, \"Official daily price exists; public purchase price is close\", does not say close to what. Try: \"The Agency publishes a daily farm-gate price, and the public purchase price sits within [x]% of it.\"", "\"Three experiments could change our estimates\" sits under a bare \"Limitations\" label, and Block 1 and Block 2 both end with a bare \"Conclusion\" label at the same level as section 5's Conclusion heading. Three headings called Conclusion on one page is one too many; call the inner ones \"Block 1 conclusion\" and \"Block 2 conclusion\".", "The References cite \"Geospatial Analysis: capital cost NT$30,000 per unit ... and the salt-affected profit figures behind the 2.4-year payback\" with no note that the two pages value a stressed season differently. Publish the disagreement or reconcile it; citing the other page silently is the one option that costs credibility.", "\"ACC deaminase kinetics: Pseudomonas putida UW4, Km 3.4 mM, kcat 146 min−1\" has no citation, and kcat 146 carries the 54 nM and 1.96 mg L−1 figures. Add the paper and a DOI. Only one reference on the page has a DOI.", "Figure 21's panel uses 17 twice in four lines, once for the quiet seasons and once for the worst-case application count. Write \"17 of 25 seasons\" and \"17 applications in 2001 and 2008\" in full so the two cannot be read as the same number.", "\"its four stops are the only four outcomes that occur in 25 years of 雲林 record\" never lists the four. Add them with their counts, the way Figure 21 and Figure 22 give 17 of 25 and 2 of 25.", "\"Block 3 also finds the first crop never crosses the release threshold in 25 years\" rests on a dekad rebuild the Methods describe for the second crop only. Say that the first crop was rebuilt too, or move the claim to where it was computed.", "\"waterlogging in the second rice crop, which is ... the one this enzyme acts on most strongly\" has no number behind it. The only efficacy you measured, 0.58, is for salinity. Give waterlogging its own efficacy or soften the claim.", "\"Our Arabidopsis results already reach the ceiling of our efficacy calculation\" does not say what the ceiling is or which result reaches it. Name the number."]}, "live": "https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/", "mockPages": ["model", "software", "protein-design", "protein-design/methodology", "protein-design/sprints", "protein-design/molecular-dynamics"], "wiki": "f036343, 2026-10-12", "root": "../", "notes": [{"id": "mm-a01", "type": "suggest", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "The model turns Taiwan’s weather into a stress forecast, the forecast into a lamp setting", "title": "Say what the model leaves out", "body": "The lead says what the chain takes in and what it gives out, which is the right shape for a first paragraph. It never says what the chain does not cover. A judge who reads only this sheet will not learn that the index does not carry salinity, that no rice plant has been treated, or that the reactor titre is one assay well. Add one sentence of scope after this one.", "after": "It covers rice in Taiwan, waterlogging in the second crop and one reactor strain. It does not cover salinity inside the index, any dose measured on rice, or a field trial.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02"], "excerpt_note": "Three bullets fix the scope of Heidelberg's whole page in about forty words, and the third one is the boundary: what the model assumes.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-a02", "type": "mistake", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "Three blocks run as one chain, each tested against something outside itself.", "title": "Only Block one was tested outside itself", "body": "Your own conclusions disagree with this line. Block 2 says the activity \"came from one well and falls below the assay's quantification limit\" and that its light curve comes from published reporter measurements. Figure 23 says the canopy correction is \"a model, not a measurement\". Only Block 1 was scored against data it had not seen. Claim that for Block 1 and say plainly what Blocks 2 and 3 rest on.", "after": "Three blocks run as one chain. Block 1 was scored on harvests it had not seen. Block 2 rests on one assay well and a published light curve, and Block 3 on one plate and an unmeasured canopy correction.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-a03", "type": "mistake", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "the one kept has 32% less error than the no-weather trend in the second crop", "title": "The headline number is not on the page", "body": "This is the first number a judge meets and the page never shows the figures behind it. Block 1's only error comparison is in the Figure 11 caption, \"county error: trend 419, model 365 kg/ha\", and that is the first crop and about 13%. Either put the second-crop error pair into Result 3 so a reader can check 32%, or quote the first-crop numbers here instead.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-a04", "type": "mistake", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "which a rice canopy multiplies to 4,008 L of culture per hectare per application", "title": "One quantity, two numbers", "body": "This sheet says 4,008 L/ha. The Block 3 assumption fold, the page conclusion and \"What would overturn this\" all say 4,082 L/ha. Keep 4,082, which is the chain with the unrounded 2.444 canopy correction, and change this line to match. The figure images were exported with 2.4 and need re-exporting.", "after": "which a rice canopy multiplies to 4,082 L of culture per hectare per application", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-a05", "type": "clarify", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "Capital returns in 2.4 years on salt-affected coastal land; on an ordinary paddy it does not return in 64.7.", "title": "Finish the second half of the sentence", "body": "\"It does not return in 64.7\" drops the unit, and 64.7 years is the payback period itself, not a span over which nothing comes back. Give both numbers the same shape.", "after": "Capital pays back in 2.4 years on salt-affected coastal land and in 64.7 years on an ordinary paddy.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-a06", "type": "mistake", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "32% less error than the trend in the second crop", "title": "Validated is the one word Block one refuses", "body": "Your Block 1 conclusion says the index is \"a historically tested estimate of crop stress and potential protectant demand, not proof that ACC deaminase will prevent the predicted loss\". This row promises more than that, and it is one of the first words a judge reads. Use the conclusion's own wording.", "after": "- Stress index · Historically tested · 32% less error than the trend in the second crop", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-a07", "type": "clarify", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "Given before the salt, ACCD plates hold 2.6× the buffer control", "title": "Say how many plates this rests on", "body": "The row is marked Measured and gives no replicate count. Your own table also shows ACCD given after the salt reaching 2.2 times the buffer control against 2.6 for before, so the timing effect is smaller than \"Apply it first\" suggests. Add the n and the two ratios so the claim carries its own evidence.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-a08", "type": "mistake", "zone": "abstract", "anchor": "4,008 L of culture per application", "title": "Upper bound is the label you withdrew", "body": "\"What would overturn this\" says 4,082 L/ha should be read as a modelled scenario, \"neither a validated requirement nor an established upper bound\". This row calls it an upper bound. Fix the label here and keep the sentence there.", "after": "- Dose per hectare · Modelled scenario · 4,082 L of culture per application, 400 L in the tank", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-001", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Abiotic stress (heat, soil salinity, and vapor pressure deficit) reduces crop yields", "title": "The opening names three stresses you dropped", "body": "Heat, soil salinity and vapor pressure deficit are the three stresses named here, and the page then models none of them as a headline term. Result 1 is titled \"heat earns nothing\". Section 5 says of salinity that \"Block 1 does not carry it in the index\". Vapor pressure deficit never appears again. Open on the stresses the index actually carries.", "after": "Typhoon wind, badly timed rain and low light cut rice yields across Taiwan's farmland long before visible damage appears. Soil salinity does the same, and our index cannot yet see it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-002", "type": "cut", "anchor": "by disrupting plant hormone balance and protein stability at the cellular level", "title": "Cut the mechanism from the first line", "body": "This clause explains cell biology the reader has not asked for yet, and it pushes the first number out of the opening. Every winner's first paragraph attaches a number to the problem, and you have one four lines below: county rice yields for every season since 2001.", "after": "Abiotic stress cuts crop yields across Taiwan's farmland long before visible damage appears. Taiwan's county rice records since 2001 let us put a size on it.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-01"], "excerpt_note": "Four sentences: the gap stated as an absence anyone can check, the claim, the method, then the size of the data. No adjective is doing any work."}, {"id": "mm-003", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "that senses field stress in real time and produces tailored protectant proteins", "title": "Real time is not what Block two found", "body": "Block 2's Result 2 is titled \"the controller must run on forecast\", and Block 3 says \"The reactor cannot chase the weather.\" Seven point three hours pass between the decision and active enzyme in the leaf, and a ten unit fleet needs 4.5 days per hectare. \"In real time\" is the claim your own page spends two sections withdrawing. Also, the model covers one protein, so \"proteins\" should be singular.", "after": "ReLEAF addresses this with an optogenetically controlled B. subtilis bioreactor that reads a weather forecast, runs ahead of the stress and holds stock of ACC deaminase.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-004", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "TakesActivity per litre, release dekads, efficacy ε", "title": "Define dekad and epsilon on the card", "body": "This card is the first place a reader meets \"dekad\" and \"ε\". The gloss for dekad arrives about three hundred lines later, inside Block 3, and ε is only defined in a Methods fold. Four words each, here, saves a judge two scroll-backs.", "after": "Takes: activity per litre, release dekads (10 day periods), efficacy ε (the share of a loss the enzyme can save)", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-005", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "GivesLitres per hectare per application, applications per season, fleet lead time, NT$ per hectare per year", "title": "Add an assumes line to each card", "body": "The three cards say what each block takes and gives, which is clearer than most winners manage. None of them says what the block assumes. One line per card would put the boundary in front of a judge before the first equation: Block 1 assumes a trend deviation is weather, Block 2 assumes a reporter curve describes acdS, Block 3 assumes an unmeasured canopy correction.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02"], "excerpt_note": "The third bullet is the whole move: \"Assumes only Newtonian mechanics and conventional base pair interactions.\"", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-006", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Most crop stress indices add temperature, water and light with weights chosen by experts", "title": "Give this claim one citation", "body": "This sentence is the reason your whole approach exists, and it is the only claim about other people's work on the page with nothing behind it. The References list has no entry for it. Name one or two expert-weighted indices and cite them, so the gap you are filling can be checked.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-007", "type": "praise", "anchor": "If weather alone can predict which seasons fall, and by how much, that prediction is a stress index", "title": "This sentence earns the whole block", "body": "In one line you turn a definition into a test. Because the index is defined through yield loss, every past harvest becomes a held-out check, and a judge sees that before any equation appears. Keep this sentence word for word.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-01"], "excerpt_note": "The same discipline from the other side: UToronto states the gap as an absence anyone can falsify, then claims only what the data can carry.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-008", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Result 1 · Light, wind and badly timed water cost rice; heat earns nothing", "title": "The heading and the table disagree on heat", "body": "This heading says heat earns nothing. The Kept column for the first crop lists \"warm nights, hours ≥ 35 °C\", which are both heat terms. Figure 4 then says shuffling warm nights \"costs nothing\". Three parts of one section give three answers. Decide which is right and make the other two follow it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-009", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "We gathered 18 candidate stresses", "title": "Eighteen gathered, fifteen searched, three unexplained", "body": "You gather 18 candidates and then search every combination of 15, and the three that fell out before the search are never named. The next sentence says each candidate \"was then judged three ways\" and the three tests are never named either. Both gaps are one short clause each.", "after": "We gathered 18 candidate stresses and carried 15 into the search, dropping [three names] because [reason]. Each candidate was then judged three ways: [test], [test] and [test].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-010", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Stars mark student-model variables.", "title": "Explain student model and instructor model", "body": "\"Student model\" appears here with no definition, and the Figure 6 caption then adds \"instructor model (v3)\" and \"v-county\". A judge reading an iGEM page will stop at that pair and wonder who built which one. Say in one sentence what the two models are, who fitted each and why both are shown.", "after": "Stars mark the variables the team proposed. [Who] fitted the team model and [who] fitted the comparison model; both are shown because [reason].", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-011", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Shuffling rain by growth stage costs 19.8 kg/ha (p = 0.010)", "title": "You printed your own null results", "body": "The caption gives the effect, the unit and the p-value, and then reports that two of your own proposed variables are worth nothing. A judge believes the first half because you published the second. Carry this habit into the Block 2 and Block 3 captions.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-og-heidelberg-02"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg scores its own binder generator against a wet lab ranking and prints the negative correlation, τ = −0.20, next to the positive ones.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-012", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Which stresses were supported by the data.", "title": "This paragraph repeats the one above", "body": "Everything here is already in \"Each candidate was then judged three ways\" and in the table caption underneath. Cut the paragraph and let the table do its job.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-013", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Second crop: a normal year sits near 0.33", "title": "Put the second crop in the table", "body": "The first crop gets a seven row table with kilograms, New Taiwan dollars and a risk column. The second crop gets four numbers in a footnote, and the second crop is the one Block 3 actually uses. Give it the same shape.", "after": "Second crop, same table: index 0.33 is a normal year, 0.5 costs 5% of yield, 0.6 costs 9%, 0.8 costs 15%. [Add the kg/ha, NT$/ha and risk columns from your own fit.]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-014", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The values in Figure 11 come from the literature.", "title": "Wrong figure number", "body": "The efficacy values are in Figure 10. Figure 11 is the island yield chart in Result 3. A judge who follows the pointer lands on the wrong picture and reads the next three sentences against it.", "after": "The values in Figure 10 come from the literature.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-015", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Arabidopsis fresh weight at 75 millimolar sodium chloride, with and without ACC deaminase at three timings", "title": "The middle column reads backwards", "body": "\"Change under salt (vs same treatment, 0 mM)\" is +35% for ACCD before salt and +42% for ACCD after salt, so at a glance the column says salt helped those plates. Say in the caption what that column compares and why two of its values are positive, and add the replicate count.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-016", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "About 58% of the yield a salty paddy loses could be saved", "title": "Two different efficacies, no bridge", "body": "This caption and the Methods fold both use 0.58 for salinity. The payback table uses 0.28 and the text beside it jumps to a ceiling of 1.0. Nothing on the page says where 0.28 came from, and the whole payback rests on it. Give 0.28 a source, or use 0.58 and redo the table.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-017", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The reference is the trend: each county's yield trend with no weather at all", "title": "You named your no skill line", "body": "Naming the baseline that any weather model must beat, and then printing both errors side by side, is what makes a model score mean something. Of the six Best Model winners only one anchors on an external baseline at all, and less carefully than this.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-018", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "county error: trend 419, model 365 kg/ha", "title": "Add the second crop error pair", "body": "These two numbers are the only error comparison on the page, and they are first crop. The abstract sheet and the page conclusion both lead with 32% less error in the second crop, which a reader cannot check anywhere. Put the second-crop trend and model errors here, in the same kg/ha form.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-019", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Across the historical rice data, the weather linked to yield loss differed between the two crops.", "title": "Lead the conclusion with the finding", "body": "This opening sentence says a difference exists and makes the reader wait two sentences to learn what it is. Your second sentence already carries it. Swap them.", "after": "For the second crop, the strongest signals came from typhoon-related wind and lodging, low light, and water-related stress, and heat and warm nights added little once those were considered. The first crop behaves differently, and its index was less informative.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-020", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Block 1 therefore provides a historically tested estimate of crop stress and potential protectant demand", "title": "The strongest sentence in Block one", "body": "You state what the block does establish and then the claim you are not making, in one line. Many model pages stop before the second half. This is also the sentence your abstract sheet contradicts by calling the index Validated, so change the sheet and keep this.", "excerpts": ["model-2023-og-toronto-03"], "excerpt_note": "Toronto takes one of its own headline targets and shows the method probably produced it. Same discipline, applied to a result the team would rather keep.", "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "mm-021", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "One litre of culture clears a gram of ACC a day.", "title": "The deck promises more than Result three", "body": "This line states a production capacity as settled fact. The section's own Result 3 is titled \"Secretion detected at 7.25 mU mL−1, not yet quantified\", and the figure behind it is one well below the assay's quantitation limit. A deck can carry the number if it carries the limit in the same breath.", "after": "One litre of culture cleared about a gram of ACC a day in our single assay well, below the level at which we can quantify it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-022", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "The construct supplies phycocyanobilin.", "title": "Say whether your construct carries them", "body": "The basis column explains that Castillo-Hair co-expressed ho1 and pcyA. It does not say whether your construct carries them, and every light setpoint on the page depends on it. Add one sentence naming the part numbers that carry ho1 and pcyA in your build and how you confirmed they are present.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "mm-023", "type": "praise", "anchor": "so the parameters are host-specific and the B. subtilis values are the correct ones here", "title": "You showed why the host matters", "body": "You give the borrowed constants with their errors, then a second host's values to show that choosing the wrong set would move the lamp about four-fold. That is how to inherit a parameter instead of copying it. The other borrowed constants on the page, kcat 146 and efficacy 0.58, would gain from the same treatment.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-024", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Both quantities are per unit area, so no area term enters.", "title": "Move the ceiling up to the figure", "body": "This paragraph does three unrelated jobs: it derives the lux conversion, it defends the units, and it states where the promoter saturates. The saturation sentence belongs beside Figure 15, where the reader first meets the 0.94 ceiling. Leave the conversion and the unit check here.", "after": "[Keep the first two sentences here. Move \"The ceiling saturates the promoter at f = 0.94, so demands above that are indistinguishable to the reactor\" up into the paragraph that introduces Figure 15.]", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-025", "type": "praise", "anchor": "activation is slower than deactivation, so the system commits to production before it can confirm the stress", "title": "A caption that states a consequence", "body": "Most captions name the object. This one reads the shape of the curve and tells the reader what it costs the controller. The best winner captions do exactly this, and most of yours already do. Keep the habit.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "mm-026", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The five steps between deciding to produce and the enzyme being active in the leaf", "title": "The hours column does not add up", "body": "1.8 plus 2 plus 0.5 plus 0.1 plus 3 is 7.4, and the Total H row says 7.3. The reactor-only note says 4.2 h where 1.8 plus 2 plus 0.5 is 4.3. Both totals are quoted elsewhere, including the abstract sheet and the lead-time arithmetic, so either fix the column or show the unrounded values that add to 7.3.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-027", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Measured activity. ACC deaminase activity was measured by DNPH colorimetry on culture supernatant.", "title": "Make these run in labels real headings", "body": "Result 3 is carried by six run-in labels: Measured activity, Signal is not a biomass artefact, Calibration correction, Implied enzyme concentration, Accumulation, and one unlabelled paragraph. Nowhere else on the page uses this device, so the reader loses the thread. Promote them to small headings, or drop the labels and let each first sentence carry its paragraph.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-028", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "WB800 has the highest OD600 and the second-highest absorbance", "title": "The absorbance rank is the other way round", "body": "The table above reads A540 0.388 for WB800, 0.404 for csn and 0.423 for samyQ, so WB800 has the lowest absorbance of the three. The slip costs you evidence: highest biomass with the lowest absorbance refutes a density artefact more completely than second-highest would.", "after": "WB800 has the highest OD600 and the lowest absorbance; samyQ has lower biomass and higher signal.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-029", "type": "praise", "anchor": "This argument is independent of the standard curve and survives any calibration error.", "title": "An argument that outlives your calibration", "body": "You found a check that does not depend on the number you are least sure of, and you said so in one line. This is the best sentence in Block 2 and it is worth more to a judge than the titre itself. Correct the absorbance rank above and the paragraph is airtight.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-ug-heidelberg-04"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg runs a second, independent check on a trajectory and lets it overturn an agreement the error bars seemed to allow.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-030", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Calibration correction. The standard-curve concentrations were originally computed against a 110 µL reaction volume", "title": "Correct the number before you quote it", "body": "Finding and publishing a 46% correction to your own numbers is a move none of the six winners makes, so this paragraph deserves to come earlier. As it stands, a reader has already met 7.25 mU mL−1 in a heading, a figure and a table before learning that every derived activity moves by a factor of 1.460. Put this paragraph before Figure 17.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-031", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Implied enzyme concentration. Substrate was 43.0 mM against a Km of 3.4 mM", "title": "Say which correction is already in the titre", "body": "This is the second \"correcting\" in three paragraphs and it is a different one: here you divide by 0.927 for substrate saturation, above you divided by 1.460 for the standard curve. Every number downstream, as far as 1.056 g per day and the litres per hectare, uses 7.25 straight. One sentence saying whether 7.25 is before or after the 1.460 correction also decides whether it still clears the 4.9 detection limit.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-032", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "At a doubling time of 29 min in LB at 37 °C", "title": "Digital Twin measured this, and differently", "body": "Our Digital Twin page measures WB800 itself: 139 min on the light-programmable array, 134 min in an unstirred flask and 35 min in the stirred reactor. It then warns that \"a prior has to be labelled with its vessel and its mixing\". A borrowed 29 min with no vessel named sits against our own data, and at 139 min this batch runs about 20 h instead of 4 to 6. Use our measurement and name the vessel.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-033", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Batch duration was not recorded, so the production rate is inferred rather than measured.", "title": "You labelled your own missing datum", "body": "A reader could not have discovered this, and you volunteered it in eleven words at the end of the paragraph that needs it. Keep the sentence, and use the same construction for the medium half-life and the plate area.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-034", "type": "praise", "anchor": "it came from one well and falls below the assay's quantification limit", "title": "The number with its n and its limit", "body": "This clause gives the figure, the replicate count behind it and the thing it cannot show. Then you name the response time as \"a planning scenario, not a validated operating time\". A judge who reads this trusts the rest of your numbers more.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-og-heidelberg-03"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg adds one more beat: what accuracy the model actually needs to be useful. That sentence is the one thing this paragraph still lacks.", "ballot": "Did the team use experimental or other relevant data to develop, train, or validate the model or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-035", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The figures and tables in this block carry 4,008 L/ha, which is the same chain with the canopy correction rounded to 2.4", "title": "Pick one number and re-export", "body": "The sentence is honest and a judge will still see one quantity printed as two numbers across the page. Keep 4,082 L/ha, the unrounded 2.444 chain the surrounding text and the conclusion already use. Then re-export Figures 20, 21 and 22 and the land-size and basis tables with 4,082, change the abstract sheet, and delete this sentence.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-036", "type": "cut", "anchor": "This is the most important result.", "title": "Cut the directions to your own page", "body": "The first sentence claims importance instead of showing it, and the second tells the reader your sections run in an unusual order, which invites the question why. The heading above already says what this result is.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-037", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "A farmer with 1 分 and a farmer with 10 公頁 face the identical calculation.", "title": "The character for hectare is wrong", "body": "公頁 is not a unit. The word is 公頃. It appears here and three times in the land-size table, while the calculator panel in Figure 21 has 公頃 correctly. A Taiwanese judge will see this on the first read.", "after": "A farmer with 1 分 and a farmer with 10 公頃 face the identical calculation.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-038", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Litres of neat culture. One application is diluted into 400 L of tank mix per hectare.", "title": "The tank volume is stated six times", "body": "400 L of tank mix appears in Figure 20, in this footnote, in both calculator figures, in the page conclusion and on the abstract sheet. The table header above already says \"One application\". Cut the footnote.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-039", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "A recorded volume over a known area is a rate", "title": "The area is the part you reconstructed", "body": "Your own table one paragraph below reads \"Plate area | 100 cm2 | Reconstructed, never written down | Assumed\". Calling the area known here undoes the provenance column that is the best thing in this section.", "after": "A recorded volume over an assumed plate area is a rate, and a rate scales to a hectare without any modelling in between.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-040", "type": "praise", "anchor": "One plate with neat, 1:3, 1:10 and 1:30 arms would find it", "title": "You named the plate that would fix it", "body": "A ceiling admitted, the exact experiment that would replace it, the dilution arms spelled out, and the warning that every litre on the page moves with the answer. This is the clearest statement of a next step anywhere on our wiki.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-04"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto's four beats are result, diagnosis, fix, and why the fix is blocked. Yours runs the first three and does not need the fourth.", "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-041", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Applications per season and the culture they need, on four bases, with what each basis is good for", "title": "Every basis says what it is for", "body": "Four bases, each with the decision it serves, and the mean labelled \"Budgeting only. No season ever looks like this\". Refusing your own average inside the table that reports it stops a judge quoting 3.28 passes back at you. No winner's parameter table does this.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-042", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "This does not enter the arithmetic, because whatever uptake happened on the plate is already inside the 1,670 L.", "title": "Give this section a clearer place", "body": "This is the sharpest reasoning in Block 3 and it sits at the end of \"Where those numbers come from\", a section about numbers it says it is not part of. \"This\" also points back at the heading. Open on the question and the section stands up by itself.", "after": "Can an intact enzyme cross into a rice leaf? Uptake does not enter the arithmetic, because whatever happened on the plate is already inside the 1,670 L. It matters for a different reason. If the enzyme cannot cross, no amount of volume fixes it, and the canopy correction is correcting the wrong thing.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-043", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "stomata on both leaf surfaces, upper density comparable to or slightly above the lower", "title": "Your own figures say the opposite", "body": "Figure 25 gives 186.7 stomata per mm2 on the upper surface and Figure 26 gives 621.0 on the lower, so the upper is about three times sparser. The References repeat 186.7 adaxial against 621.0 abaxial. This is the sentence that justifies spraying the upper surface, so correcting it changes the two sentences after it.", "after": "Rice is amphistomatous: stomata sit on both surfaces, about 187 per mm2 above and 621 below. Most crops keep theirs only on the underside, where a spray never lands, so a sprayed rice leaf at least offers pores.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-044", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "which is 0.2083 reactor-litre-days for every litre of culture", "title": "Two reciprocals in one sentence", "body": "A judge has to invert 34,807 mU per reactor-litre per day and then hold \"reactor-litre-days per litre of culture\" in mind before the lead-time table makes sense. State it as a plain production rate.", "after": "Block 2 gives the reactor 34,807 mU per reactor-litre per day at our titre. At that rate one reactor-litre makes about 4.8 litres of culture a day. Lead time for one application is the culture volume divided by total vessel volume, plus the 7.3 h forecast lag.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-045", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Storage stability is therefore load bearing, and it is unmeasured.", "title": "You named the beam you have not tested", "body": "The paragraph takes its own result, 4.5 days against a 3-day forecast, and rebuilds the concept of operation around it: brew through the season, spend from stock. Then it points at the single assumption that new design rests on and says it is unmeasured. That last clause is what makes the paragraph credible.", "excerpts": ["model-2023-og-toronto-02"], "excerpt_note": "Toronto lets a constraint from the bench change the model. Here a result from the model changes how the hardware would be run, which is the same loop running the other way.", "ballot": "Did the model or computational approach help the team understand, predict, or design a part, device, or system?"}, {"id": "mm-046", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "A 5-year payback would need an efficacy of 12.8", "title": "That number is a multiple of efficacy", "body": "Efficacy is defined on this page as a share between 0 and 1, so 12.8 cannot be one. Working from your own table, a 5-year payback needs about NT$60,000 a year, which is 3.6 times the 0.28 case or 7.7 times the 1.0 case. Say which calculation produced 12.8 and give it a unit. The closing clause is also a comma splice.", "after": "A 5-year payback would need about NT$60,000 of benefit a year, roughly [8] times what an efficacy of 1.0 can deliver. That is above the physical ceiling.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-047", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Annual benefit and payback period on ordinary and salt-affected land, at two efficacies", "title": "Benefit does not scale on one half", "body": "The salt-affected rows scale with efficacy: 126,967 at 0.28 against 444,724 at 1.00 is a ratio of 0.285. The ordinary-paddy rows do not: 4,635 at 0.28 against 7,824 at 1.00 is a ratio of 0.59. At 0.28 the ordinary benefit should be about 2,191, which puts the payback near 137 years instead of 64.7. The text above also says NT$4,429 where this table says 4,635. Your headline result depends on which of these is right.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-048", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Three experiments could change our estimates substantially", "title": "Give the page its own limitations section", "body": "These three experiments are the best limitations list on the page, and they sit in a fold inside Block 3, so they read as limits on Block 3 alone. Blocks 1 and 2 have open items of their own: the index does not carry salinity, the titre is one well, the response horizon holds an assumed placeholder. Collect them into one top-level Limitations section before the Conclusion and name who runs each experiment.", "excerpts": ["model-2023-og-toronto-01"], "excerpt_note": "Toronto separates what has been validated from what has not in two short sentences, and says experimental validation will follow.", "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-049", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Where improvements need to be made", "title": "Rename this to what it holds", "body": "The heading promises future improvements and the section delivers an errata list for mistakes you already caught. Those are different things, and the errata is the more impressive one. Name it for what it is, and move it up beside the Limitations section instead of leaving it at the foot of Block 3.", "after": "Errors we caught before publishing", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-050", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Four errors survived into drafts of this page before we caught them.", "title": "Four promised, one delivered", "body": "The table below has one row and its caption reads \"An error caught in an earlier draft of this block\". A judge who counts will read the gap as three errors you chose not to show. Either recover the other three from your drafts or say one here, and keep the sentence that follows.", "after": "One error survived into a draft of this page before we caught it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-051", "type": "praise", "anchor": "We list them because a model that has never been wrong in public has usually not been checked.", "title": "The best line on the page", "body": "Eighteen words that give a judge a reason to trust everything above them. It is also a standard the rest of our wiki can be held to. Keep it exactly, and make the count in the sentence before it true.", "excerpts": ["model-2023-og-toronto-03"], "excerpt_note": "Toronto publishes the weakness of one of its own headline predictions. Printing the error is the move; this sentence goes one further and explains why you print them.", "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-052", "type": "cut", "anchor": "The draft writes out one of the four.", "title": "Do not advertise the missing three", "body": "This paragraph tells the reader that three quarters of the errata is lost. It helps nobody and it undercuts the section's own point. Fix the count in the opening sentence and cut this.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-053", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Measuring rice leaf area and spray capture directly would replace the canopy correction, which alone spans 1.68 to 3.52.", "title": "You said what would overturn you", "body": "A falsification condition, the measurement that would settle it, and the width of the band it would replace, in one sentence. The clause after it then refuses both labels a reader might reach for. None of the six winners has a section in this shape.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-og-heidelberg-03"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg adds the one beat missing here: the level of accuracy the model actually needs in order to be useful.", "ballot": "How impressive is the modeling or computational approach?"}, {"id": "mm-054", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Given that the crop is rice, how much protectant does a farmer need for a field of a given size?", "title": "Ask the question the way a farmer would", "body": "\"Given that the crop is rice\" and \"of a given size\" are hedges in front of a simple question, and this is the question the whole page exists to answer. Say it plainly and let the next sentence carry the hedges.", "after": "How much protectant does one hectare of rice need?", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-055", "type": "cut", "anchor": "A number on its own is not a model.", "title": "Cut the aphorism", "body": "The sentence after it makes the same point with a reason attached. One of the two can go, and this is the one that carries no information.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-056", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The payback in section 4 is priced on salinity. The spray schedule in section 3 is counted from waterlogging dekads.", "title": "Both of these live in one section", "body": "The spray schedule is \"How often a field actually needs treating\", which is inside Block 3, the same section as the payback. Section 3 is Block 2, optogenetic kinetics. Name the blocks instead of the section numbers, because the page numbers Block 1 as section 2 and a reader cannot tell which scheme you mean.", "after": "The payback is priced on salinity. The spray schedule, in the same block, is counted from waterlogging dekads.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-057", "type": "praise", "anchor": "We know this is the largest open problem in the model.", "title": "You published the mismatch you cannot fix", "body": "Two different stresses carry the two halves of Block 3, you say which is which, you cost the fix at about 15 applications over a 30-day window, and you do not pretend it is solved. None of the six Best Model winners admits an unresolved internal mismatch like this. Fix the section numbers in the first two sentences and leave the rest alone.", "excerpts": ["model-2024-og-utoronto-02"], "excerpt_note": "\"None of the oris in this batch worked.\" A failure given its own sentence instead of a clause. Same courage, different subject.", "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-058", "type": "cut", "anchor": "For us, that is the purpose of the model", "title": "The last sentence repeats the first two", "body": "\"We began by trying to calculate\" and \"We ended up discovering how much we still need to learn\" already say this. Ending on your own two sentences is stronger than explaining them.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "mm-059", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "County rice yield statistics, every season from 2001", "title": "Release the code with the data", "body": "You name the data source and nothing another team could run. The 32,767-model scoring run, the per-crop fit, the leave-one-out indices and the frozen 2026 forecast file are all reusable, and three of the six Best Model winners publish a repository. Add a link and a version number for whatever you fitted with.", "excerpts": ["model-2025-og-heidelberg-04"], "excerpt_note": "Heidelberg writes its extension recipe for a stranger: what table to supply, what the pipeline does with it, and what a new component would unlock.", "ballot": "Does the modeling or computational approach provide a good example for others?"}, {"id": "mm-060", "type": "cut", "anchor": "ACC deaminase quaternary structure: homotetramer, about 144 kDa, 36.5 kDa per subunit", "title": "This reference is listed twice", "body": "The Block 2 list already carries it as \"homotetramer, ~144 kDa, 36.5 kDa per subunit\". Keep one copy, in Block 3 where the 9.4 nm size is used, and give it a source. Neither copy cites anything at present.", "excerpts": []}], "excerpts": {"model-2023-og-toronto-01": {"id": "model-2023-og-toronto-01", "prize": "model", "team": "Toronto", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/toronto/model", "section": "1. Purpose", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Escherichia coli can be genetically modified to uptake methane from landfill sites, mitigating the pollutive impact of methane production. The Dry Lab division aims to provide genetic addition, knock-out, and over-expression targets for improved methane uptake. We made use of well-established modelling and simulation tools such as Flux Balance Analysis as well novel implementations of scanning algorithms to produce results. The key targets reported are frdABCD (knockout), MEOHDH, MEOHtrpp, MEOHtex(overexpression), FDH (addition). These results were validated through literature. Experimental validation will follow.", "why_it_works": "The opening names the deliverable the dry lab owes the wet lab, lists the five gene targets by name, and then separates what has been validated from what has not in two short sentences.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2023-og-toronto-02": {"id": "model-2023-og-toronto-02", "prize": "model", "team": "Toronto", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/toronto/model", "section": "3.1. Motivation", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "Additionally, the implementation of many algorithms was primarily optimized for producing helpful and productive results for experimental teams. For example, the knockout algorithm (Section ???) prioritizes increased granularity in results in order to clearly distinguish between the top identified knockout targets. This was motivated by the fact that due to time limitations, the experimental team could only test one knockout prediction, and higher certainty and distinction between targets was greatly valuable.", "why_it_works": "The wet lab's budget changes the algorithm: the experimental team could test one knockout, so the ranking was built to separate the top candidates sharply. A constraint from the bench is given as the reason for a design choice inside the model.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2023-og-toronto-03": {"id": "model-2023-og-toronto-03", "prize": "model", "team": "Toronto", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/toronto/model", "section": "5.2 Limitations and Applications", "function": "limitation", "text": "In the second case, which is, in fact, one of the top targets for overexpression predicted by our algorithms, a small increase in fluxes through Triose-phosphate isomerase (TPI) correlates with the highest growth rates. However, as can be observed from Figure 6, this relationship is not uniformly positive throughout the entire possible flux ranges. This makes TPI a less than ideal candidate for overexpression than what FSEOF suggests.", "why_it_works": "The team takes one of its own headline overexpression targets and shows it is probably an artefact of the method. Admitting that a published result of yours is weak, in the section where a reader will look for it, is the strongest credibility move on any of these pages.", "teaches": ["uncertainty", "evidence", "voice"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-01": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-01", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Abstract", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Plasmids are essential vectors in synthetic biology, facilitating the delivery of genetic material in bacterial systems. However, the generation of novel plasmid sequences with targeted functionalities presents a significant challenge, and no experimentally validated models exist for reliably designing biologically viable, de novo plasmids. We present a novel plasmid generation pipeline that integrates machine learning with experimental validation to produce functional plasmid sequences. Utilizing a custom Byte Pair Encoding tokenizer and the Mamba2 state-space model architecture, our model efficiently handles the long and complex nature of plasmid DNA. The model was trained in two phases: pre-training on a comprehensive dataset of over 137,000 plasmid sequences, followed by fine-tuning on specific target sequences.", "why_it_works": "The gap is stated as an absence that can be checked (no experimentally validated models exist), then the claim, then the architecture, then the size of the training set. Four moves, no adjectives doing the work.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-02": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-02", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Batch 1", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "This encouraged us to dive deeper into the batch and select top sequences for in vitro validation. An initial set of 6 oris, a mix of Class A and Class B was selected. However, after attempting to design these plasmids in Wet Lab, Class A plasmids were deemed to be too difficult to test at this stage, as their machinery is more complex and difficult to detect. This Wet Lab - Dry Lab iteration meant that instead 5 promising Class B oris were delivered to Wet Lab. Finally, one promising protein was also selected. None of the oris in this batch worked.", "why_it_works": "The wet lab sends a prediction back: Class A plasmids are too hard to test, so the shortlist is rebuilt and the reason is given. Then the six-word verdict on the whole batch. The failure gets its own sentence instead of being buried in a clause.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "voice", "concision"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-og-utoronto-04": {"id": "model-2024-og-utoronto-04", "prize": "model", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/model", "section": "Key Ori Features and Classes", "function": "limitation", "text": "These negative scores suggest poor alignment between the generated sequences and the class B elements.\nThese negative scores are likely due to a lack of sequence variability in the class B elements that were used. For example, only one version of the P2 promoter and RNA II region (From E. coli ColE1 plasmid) were used, limiting the accuracy of the alignments. Using a set of variants for each sequence could improve alignment scores. However, the lack of established consequence sequences for a P2 promoter and RNA II coding region make this a difficult next step to pursue.", "why_it_works": "A negative result, a diagnosis of why it is negative (one reference sequence rather than a set), the fix that would follow, and then the admission that the fix is blocked too. Four steps, and no retreat into 'further work is needed'.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "structure"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02": {"id": "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-02", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "DaVinci in a Nutshell", "function": "definition", "text": "- Input: staple target DNA sequence, experimental condition, and staple protein structure\n- Output stapling feasibility, staple design, and binding characteristics including binding energy and dynamics\n- Assumes only Newtonian mechanics and conventional base pair interactions", "why_it_works": "Three bullets declare what the model takes, what it returns, and what physics it assumes. The scope of a long page is fixed in about forty words, so a reader knows what not to expect from it.", "teaches": ["structure", "concision", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-04": {"id": "model-2024-ug-heidelberg-04", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Static Molecular Interaction Predictions", "function": "uncertainty", "text": "For this construct, we observed a positive ΔG of 175±97kCal⋅mol−1 for dissociation, suggesting that the reverse process — protein binding — occurs spontaneously over time. Comparing this result with wet lab data highlights discrepancies, as they measured ΔG=9.256±10.676⋅kCal⋅mol−1 using EMSA. This can also be compared to literature with an estimate of ΔG=10.7±11.48⋅kCal⋅mol−1 (Jessica J. Hollenbeck et al., 2001). Even though our calculation is within the 1.6 deviation of the experimental data, this is due to a large error in the calculation. Moreover, when visualizing the trajectory, DNA strand separation was observed, suggesting an unnatural event. Thus, this calculation will require further investigation to improve accuracy.", "why_it_works": "The model's number is put beside the team's own EMSA measurement and a published value, all three with error bars. The apparent agreement is then refused: it holds only because the computed error is huge. A second independent check, the trajectory, confirms the result is wrong.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "voice"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2025-og-heidelberg-02": {"id": "model-2025-og-heidelberg-02", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Results: Wet lab binder and metric validation", "function": "validation", "text": "The ranking of the experimentally validated binders was subsequently compared to BindCraft-derived i_pTM scores, as well as KD-based rankings acquired through our US simulations and Prodigy. For this, the Kendall's τ as well as Spearman correlation metric were utilized, whereas Kendall's τ is better suited for quantifying ranking similarity for the small sample size of n = 5 binders (Kendall, 1938). We found that both the US-derived ranking showed the better alignment with the experimental data than the BindCraft i_pTM ranking, with a Kendall's τUS = 0.80 compared to τBindCraft = −0.20 (Table 4), whereas Prodigy also performed relatively well with a Kendall's τProdigy = 0.40.", "why_it_works": "The in silico rankings are scored against a wet lab ranking the model never saw, and the statistic is chosen because the sample is small (n = 5) rather than because it flatters. The team's own generator scores negative, and that is printed.", "teaches": ["evidence", "uncertainty", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2025-og-heidelberg-03": {"id": "model-2025-og-heidelberg-03", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Results: Wet Lab Validation of Circuit Switch Behavior", "function": "limitation", "text": "The slight upward deviation in the fitted nH value could be due to experimental noise, minor cooperative effects not accounted for in the model, or simplifications in the model itself. However, these small discrepancies do not change the core conclusion: the system's dose-response curve is slow-switching. This level of agreement is sufficient for early-stage screening to identify and exclude designs with clearly undesirable characteristics and to prioritize architectures for more detailed in-vitro characterization.", "why_it_works": "A predicted Hill coefficient of 1.002 against a fitted 1.373 is explained by three candidate causes, one of them the model's own simplifications. Then the two sentences that keep the reader: the conclusion that survives the mismatch, and the standard of accuracy this model actually needs to be useful.", "teaches": ["uncertainty", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}, "model-2025-og-heidelberg-04": {"id": "model-2025-og-heidelberg-04", "prize": "model", "team": "Heidelberg", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model", "section": "Outlook", "function": "reuse", "text": "The current database is limited to a selection of components with measured or well-characterized parameter values, but the framework is designed to be easily extendable. The process of database generation is fully automated. Users provide a structured table with allowed parameter values, and the pipeline systematically generates all possible permutations. These combinations are then evaluated using the model, and the resulting descriptors are recorded for each configuration. To expand the database, users can simply add new components with their respective kinetic parameters, including protein binding domains for additional ligands, additional LZs for fine tuned substrate recruitment dynamics and enzymes for tailored catalytic activity. The pipeline will automatically characterize all newly generated combinations, which could uncover previously inaccessible switching behaviors.", "why_it_works": "The limitation and the hand-off are the same paragraph: here is what the database does not yet cover, here is the exact input another team supplies to extend it, and here is what the pipeline then does on its own.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "structure", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Best Model"}}};
